+{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"call_05_QuAClWqc3wpGu68snENm5456","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Factory Reset\n2\t\n3\tSolve button-toggle puzzles for a factory machine control panel.\n4\t\n5\t## Part 1: Toggle Puzzle\n6\t\n7\tA machine has N lights, all initially **off** (false). There are M buttons. Each button, when pressed, **toggles** a specific set of lights (off→on, on→off). Each button can be pressed **at most once** (0 or 1 presses).\n8\t\n9\tFind the minimum set of buttons to press so that the lights match a target configuration.\n10\t\n11\t### Data Model\n12\t\n13\t```typescript\n14\tinterface Machine {\n15\t  target: boolean[];     // desired state for each light (all start off)\n16\t  buttons: number[][];   // buttons[i] = array of light indices toggled by button i\n17\t}\n18\t```\n19\t\n20\t### API\n21\t\n22\t```typescript\n23\tfunction solveToggle(machine: Machine): number[] | null;\n24\t```\n25\t\n26\t- Return an array of **button indices** (0-based) to press, sorted in ascending order.\n27\t- If multiple solutions exist, return the one with the **fewest button presses**.\n28\t- If tied on press count, return the **lexicographically smallest** set of indices.\n29\t- Return `null` if no solution exists.\n30\t\n31\t### Example\n32\t\n33\t```\n34\tLights: 4 (indices 0-3), all start off\n35\tTarget: [true, true, false, true]\n36\tButtons:\n37\t  0: toggles lights [0, 1]\n38\t  1: toggles lights [1, 2, 3]\n39\t  2: toggles lights [0, 3]\n40\t\n41\tPress buttons 0 and 1:\n42\t  Button 0: lights become [on, on, off, off]\n43\t  Button 1: lights become [on, off, on, on]\n44\t  Wait — that gives [true, false, true, true], not the target.\n45\t\n46\tPress buttons 1 and 2:\n47\t  Button 1: [off, on, on, on]\n48\t  Button 2: [on, on, on, off]\n49\t  That gives [true, true, true, false], also wrong.\n50\t\n51\tPress buttons 0, 1, 2:\n52\t  After 0: [on, on, off, off]\n53\t  After 1: [on, off, on, on]\n54\t  After 2: [off, off, on, off]\n55\t  Wrong again.\n56\t\n57\tCorrect: press buttons 0 and 2:\n58\t  After 0: [on, on, off, off]\n59\t  After 2: [off, on, off, on]\n60\t  Result: [false, true, false, true]\n61\t  Hmm, that's not the target either.\n62\t\n63\tActually, let's re-check: target is [true, true, false, true].\n64\t  Press button 2: toggles [0, 3] → [on, off, off, on]\n65\t  Press button 1: toggles [1, 2, 3] → [on, on, on, off]\n66\t  Not matching.\n67\t\n68\t  Press button 0: toggles [0, 1] → [on, on, off, off]\n69\t  Press button 1: toggles [1, 2, 3] → [on, off, on, on]\n70\t  Press button 2: toggles [0, 3] → [off, off, on, off]\n71\t  Still wrong.\n72\t\n73\tThe toggle order doesn't matter (toggling is commutative). So for target [T, T, F, T]:\n74\tWe need light 0=on, 1=on, 2=off, 3=on.\n75\tButton 0 affects {0,1}, Button 1 affects {1,2,3}, Button 2 affects {0,3}.\n76\tWe need: b0 XOR b2 = 1 (light 0), b0 XOR b1 = 1 (light 1), b1 = 0 (light 2), b1 XOR b2 = 1 (light 3).\n77\tFrom light 2: b1=0. From light 1: b0=1. From light 0: b2=0. Check light 3: 0 XOR 0 = 0 ≠ 1. No solution!\n78\t\n79\tsolveToggle returns null.\n80\t```\n81\t\n82\t**Constraint**: Must handle up to 40 buttons and 40 lights and return results in under 5 seconds. Brute-force enumeration of all 2^40 button combinations is not feasible.\n83\t\n84\t## Part 2: Counter Puzzle\n85\t\n86\tA variant where lights are replaced by **counters**. Each counter starts at 0. Buttons **increment** (not toggle) specific counters by 1. Counters wrap around at a given modulus (e.g., mod 5 means values cycle 0→1→2→3→4→0).\n87\t\n88\tEach button can be pressed **multiple times** (0 to modulus-1 times).\n89\t\n90\t```typescript\n91\tfunction solveCounter(\n92\t  lights: number[],      // target counter values (each 0..modulus-1)\n93\t  buttons: number[][],   // buttons[i] = indices of counters affected\n94\t  modulus: number        // counters wrap at this value (always prime)\n95\t): number[] | null;\n96\t```\n97\t\n98\t- Return an array where `result[i]` is the number of times button `i` should be pressed (0 to modulus-1).\n99\t- If multiple solutions exist, return the one with the **minimum total presses** (sum of all result values).\n100\t- If tied on total, return the **lexicographically smallest** result array.\n101\t- Return `null` if no solution exists.\n102\t- The modulus is always a **prime number**.\n103\t\n104\t**Constraint**: Must handle up to 40 buttons, 40 counters, and modulus up to 97. Must complete in under 5 seconds.\n105\t\n106\t## Commands\n107\t\n108\t```bash\n109\tnpm test        # run tests\n110\tnpm run build   # compile TypeScript\n111\tnpm run lint    # lint source\n112\t```\n113\t\n114\t## File Structure\n115\t\n116\tPlace your implementation in `src/`. The main entry point should be `src/index.ts`.\n117\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9d6633c0-814e-447a-972a-967055be4a9d","uuid":"d500af33-e91d-4f20-96ff-459bd81a8140","timestamp":"2026-05-30T23:17:21.716Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/TASK.md","content":"# Factory Reset\n\nSolve button-toggle puzzles for a factory machine control panel.\n\n## Part 1: Toggle Puzzle\n\nA machine has N lights, all initially **off** (false). There are M buttons. Each button, when pressed, **toggles** a specific set of lights (off→on, on→off). Each button can be pressed **at most once** (0 or 1 presses).\n\nFind the minimum set of buttons to press so that the lights match a target configuration.\n\n### Data Model\n\n```typescript\ninterface Machine {\n  target: boolean[];     // desired state for each light (all start off)\n  buttons: number[][];   // buttons[i] = array of light indices toggled by button i\n}\n```\n\n### API\n\n```typescript\nfunction solveToggle(machine: Machine): number[] | null;\n```\n\n- Return an array of **button indices** (0-based) to press, sorted in ascending order.\n- If multiple solutions exist, return the one with the **fewest button presses**.\n- If tied on press count, return the **lexicographically smallest** set of indices.\n- Return `null` if no solution exists.\n\n### Example\n\n```\nLights: 4 (indices 0-3), all start off\nTarget: [true, true, false, true]\nButtons:\n  0: toggles lights [0, 1]\n  1: toggles lights [1, 2, 3]\n  2: toggles lights [0, 3]\n\nPress buttons 0 and 1:\n  Button 0: lights become [on, on, off, off]\n  Button 1: lights become [on, off, on, on]\n  Wait — that gives [true, false, true, true], not the target.\n\nPress buttons 1 and 2:\n  Button 1: [off, on, on, on]\n  Button 2: [on, on, on, off]\n  That gives [true, true, true, false], also wrong.\n\nPress buttons 0, 1, 2:\n  After 0: [on, on, off, off]\n  After 1: [on, off, on, on]\n  After 2: [off, off, on, off]\n  Wrong again.\n\nCorrect: press buttons 0 and 2:\n  After 0: [on, on, off, off]\n  After 2: [off, on, off, on]\n  Result: [false, true, false, true]\n  Hmm, that's not the target either.\n\nActually, let's re-check: target is [true, true, false, true].\n  Press button 2: toggles [0, 3] → [on, off, off, on]\n  Press button 1: toggles [1, 2, 3] → [on, on, on, off]\n  Not matching.\n\n  Press button 0: toggles [0, 1] → [on, on, off, off]\n  Press button 1: toggles [1, 2, 3] → [on, off, on, on]\n  Press button 2: toggles [0, 3] → [off, off, on, off]\n  Still wrong.\n\nThe toggle order doesn't matter (toggling is commutative). So for target [T, T, F, T]:\nWe need light 0=on, 1=on, 2=off, 3=on.\nButton 0 affects {0,1}, Button 1 affects {1,2,3}, Button 2 affects {0,3}.\nWe need: b0 XOR b2 = 1 (light 0), b0 XOR b1 = 1 (light 1), b1 = 0 (light 2), b1 XOR b2 = 1 (light 3).\nFrom light 2: b1=0. From light 1: b0=1. From light 0: b2=0. Check light 3: 0 XOR 0 = 0 ≠ 1. No solution!\n\nsolveToggle returns null.\n```\n\n**Constraint**: Must handle up to 40 buttons and 40 lights and return results in under 5 seconds. Brute-force enumeration of all 2^40 button combinations is not feasible.\n\n## Part 2: Counter Puzzle\n\nA variant where lights are replaced by **counters**. Each counter starts at 0. Buttons **increment** (not toggle) specific counters by 1. Counters wrap around at a given modulus (e.g., mod 5 means values cycle 0→1→2→3→4→0).\n\nEach button can be pressed **multiple times** (0 to modulus-1 times).\n\n```typescript\nfunction solveCounter(\n  lights: number[],      // target counter values (each 0..modulus-1)\n  buttons: number[][],   // buttons[i] = indices of counters affected\n  modulus: number        // counters wrap at this value (always prime)\n): number[] | null;\n```\n\n- Return an array where `result[i]` is the number of times button `i` should be pressed (0 to modulus-1).\n- If multiple solutions exist, return the one with the **minimum total presses** (sum of all result values).\n- If tied on total, return the **lexicographically smallest** result array.\n- Return `null` if no solution exists.\n- The modulus is always a **prime number**.\n\n**Constraint**: Must handle up to 40 buttons, 40 counters, and modulus up to 97. Must complete in under 5 seconds.\n\n## Commands\n\n```bash\nnpm test        # run tests\nnpm run build   # compile TypeScript\nnpm run lint    # lint source\n```\n\n## File Structure\n\nPlace your implementation in `src/`. The main entry point should be `src/index.ts`.\n","numLines":117,"startLine":1,"totalLines":117}}}
