o about 8 (p^8 = 97^8 ≈ 7.5e15 — way too big, so smaller). Actually for p=97, even k=4 gives 97^4 ≈ 88 million which might be OK. For k=3 it's 912,673 which is fine.\\n\\nBut we can't rely on the null space being small.\\n\\nAlternative approach: Use integer linear programming or dynamic programming.\\n\\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider the problem constraints. \\\"Must handle up to 40 buttons and 40 counters, and modulus up to 97. Must complete in under 5 seconds.\\\"\\n\\nMaybe the approach is:\\n1. Use Gaussian elimination to reduce the system\\n2. If the null space is small, enumerate\\n3. If the null space is large, use a different method\\n\\nFor the toggle puzzle (GF(2)), meet-in-the-middle on the buttons works perfectly: O(2^(M/2) * M).\\n\\nFor the counter puzzle with modulus p... \\n\\nOption 1: If the null space dimension is small enough, enumerate all solutions.\\nOption 2: Use meet-in-the-middle variant for GF(p).\\n\\nFor meet-in-the-middle on the counter puzzle:\\n- Split M buttons into two groups of M/2 each\\n- Enumerate all p^(M/2) combinations for each group\\n- For p=97 and M=40, p^20 is infeasible\\n\\nSo meet-in-the-middle doesn't work for the counter puzzle with large modulus.\\n\\nOption 3: Use the fact that we want to minimize sum of x_i, which is the L1 norm. We could frame this as:\\n- Find the minimum weight solution to a linear system over GF(p)\\n- This is equivalent to finding the closest vector to a particular solution in the coset of the null space, under L1 norm\\n\\nThis is a variant of the closest vector problem (CVP) in a linear code. For general p, this is NP-hard under L1 norm.\\n\\nSo the problem might be designed so that the null space is small in practice, or there might be a trick I'm missing.\\n\\nActually, let me re-read the problem more carefully. Let me look at the starter code first.\\n\\nLet me start by exploring the existing codebase.\",\"signature\":\"b94bfb71-48ad-4d47-bb09-66e35b584546\"}],\"stop_reason\":null,\