he target JS environment (ES2022, Node 16). BigInt was introduced in ES2020 and Node 10.4, so yes, it's supported. But Node 16 module resolution might have some quirks.\n\nActually, wait. I'll use BigInt for the toggle puzzle masks since we need up to 40 bits, and JavaScript numbers only support 53 bits precisely for integers. 40 bits is fine for regular numbers, but BigInt is cleaner for bit manipulation.\n\nLet me write the implementation now.\n\nFor the counter puzzle Gaussian elimination over GF(p):\n\n```typescript\n// Augmented matrix: rows = counters (N), cols = buttons (M) + 1 (RHS)\n// Each entry is in GF(p)\nfunction gaussianElimination(\n  A: number[][],  // N x M matrix over GF(p)\n  b: number[],    // RHS, length N\n  p: number       // prime modulus\n): { particular: number[] | null; nullBasis: number[][] } {\n  const N = A.length;\n  const M = A[0].length;\n  \n  // Create augmented matrix\n  const aug: number[][] = A.map((row, i) => [...row, b[i]]);\n  \n  let row = 0;\n  const pivotCol: number[] = []; // pivotCol[row] = column of pivot for row\n  const rowForPivot: Map<number, number> = new Map(); // pivot column → row\n  \n  for (let col = 0; col < M && row < N; col++) {\n    // Find a row with non-zero entry in this column\n    let pivotRow = -1;\n    for (let r = row; r < N; r++) {\n      if (aug[r][col] % p !== 0) {\n        pivotRow = r;\n        break;\n      }\n    }\n    if (pivotRow === -1) continue;\n    \n    // Swap rows\n    [aug[row], aug[pivotRow]] = [aug[pivotRow], aug[row]];\n    \n    // Normalize pivot row\n    const inv = modInverse(aug[row][col], p);\n    for (let c = col; c <= M; c++) {\n      aug[row][c] = (aug[row][c] * inv) % p;\n    }\n    \n    // Eliminate this column from all other rows\n    for (let r = 0; r < N; r++) {\n      if (r !== row && aug[r][col] !== 0) {\n        const factor = aug[r][col];\n        for (let c = col; c <= M; c++) {\n          aug[r][c] = (aug[r][c] - factor * aug[row][c]) % p;\n          if (aug[r][c] < 0) aug[r][c] += p;\n        }\n      }\n    }\n    \n    pivotCol.push(col);\n    rowForPivot.set(col, row);\n    row++;\n  }\n  \n  // Check for inconsistency: any row with all zeros in A but non-zero RHS\n  for (let r = row; r < N; r++) {\n    let allZero = true;\n    for (let c = 0; c < M; c++) {\n      if (aug[r][c] !== 0) { allZero = false; break; }\n    }\n    if (allZero && aug[r][M] % p !== 0) {\n      return { particular: null, nullBasis: [] }; // Inconsistent\n    }\n  }\n  \n  // Find particular solution (set free variables = 0)\n  const particular: number[] = new Array(M).fill(0);\n  for (let r = 0; r < pivotCol.length; r++) {\n    particular[pivotCol[r]] = aug[r][M] % p;\n    if (particular[pivotCol[r]] < 0) particular[pivotCol[r]] += p;\n  }\n  \n  // Find null space basis vectors\n  const freeVars: number[] = [];\n  for (let c = 0; c < M; c++) {\n    if (!rowForPivot.has(c)) {\n      freeVars.push(c);\n    }\n  }\n  \n  const nullBasis: number[][] = [];\n  for (let fvi = 0; fvi < freeVars.length; fvi++) {\n    const basis: number[] = new Array(M).fill(0);\n    const col = freeVars[fvi];\n    basis[col] = 1;\n    \n    // For each basic variable (pivot column), the value depends on this free variable\n    for (let r = 0; r < pivotCol.length; r++) {\n      const pivotColIdx = pivotCol[r];\n      basis[pivotColIdx] = (-aug[r][col]) % p;\n      if (basis[pivotColIdx] < 0) basis[pivotColIdx] += p;\n    }\n    \n    nullBasis.push(basis);\n  }\n  \n  return { particular, nullBasis };\n}\n```\n\nWait, this is getting complex. Let me write cleaner code.\n\nFor the null space basis, each basis vector v_j corresponds to setting the j-th free variable to 1 and all other free variables to 0. The basic variables are then determined by the RREF equations.\n\nIn RREF, if basic variable x_i has pivot in column p_i, the equation is:\nx_{p_i} + sum_{free var j} RREF[i][j] * y_j = RHS_value[i]\n\nSo x_{p_i} = RHS_value[i] - sum_{free var j} RREF[i][j] * y_j (